---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Sitrep from '../../components/Sitrep.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/vs/vscode-claude-md/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/vs/vscode-claude-md/',
    name: 'sitrep vs plain VS Code + CLAUDE.md - do you need a tool at all?',
    description:
      'An honest comparison of sitrep and the do-it-yourself default: VS Code, a file tree, and hand-maintained CLAUDE.md files. Including when the DIY setup is genuinely enough.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
  },
];

const rows = [
  {
    dim: 'What it is',
    sitrep: 'AI project status dashboard - a passive state layer over existing files',
    other: 'Your editor, a file tree, and whatever discipline you bring',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Cross-project overview',
    sitrep: 'The core feature - every project in one inbox, newest activity first',
    other: 'None - one window per workspace; state across projects lives in your head',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Where project state lives',
    sitrep: 'Extracted from the docs: status lines, phase headings, decision tables, due dates',
    other: 'In the files, unextracted - you re-read plans and scroll history to find it',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Re-entry after weeks away',
    sitrep: 'Open the inbox: what moved, what stalled, what is waiting on you',
    other: 'Open the folder, grep, scroll, reconstruct - the first fifteen minutes of the session',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Who the context serves',
    sitrep: 'You - CLAUDE.md and friends serve the agent; sitrep is the human-side view',
    other: 'The agent - CLAUDE.md briefs the next session, not the person opening the repo',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Staleness',
    sitrep: 'Surfaced - what a doc claims shown beside when it last changed',
    other: 'Invisible - a stale plan looks exactly like a current one',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Maintenance cost',
    sitrep: 'None - it parses what agents already write',
    other: 'Ongoing - CLAUDE.md files stay useful only as long as you keep editing them',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Price',
    sitrep: 'Commercial Mac app: €49 / $49 one-time, a year of updates included, keep it forever',
    other: 'Free - you already have it',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Local-first',
    sitrep: 'Yes - no account, no network, no telemetry by default',
    other: 'Yes',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sitrep vs plain VS Code + CLAUDE.md"
  description="The honest baseline comparison: do you need an AI project status dashboard at all, or is VS Code plus a hand-maintained CLAUDE.md enough? For one or two hot projects it genuinely is. Here is where the DIY setup stops scaling, concretely."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        <Sitrep /> vs plain VS&nbsp;Code&nbsp;+&nbsp;CLAUDE.md
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        The most honest comparison on this site, because the alternative is
        free and you already have it: your editor, a file tree, and
        hand-maintained context files. For one or two projects you touch
        daily, that setup is genuinely enough - and this page starts by
        saying so.
      </p>
      <p class="max-w-prose rounded-md border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Disclosure: this page is written by <a href="/about/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">the maker of sitrep</a>. Read the
        framing with that in mind - and note that where the honest answer is
        "you don't need a tool", this page says so.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="difference">
      <h2 id="difference" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The one-sentence difference</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          The DIY setup stores project state in files and in your head; you
          are the extraction layer. <Sitrep /> is an AI project status
          dashboard: a state layer on top of the markdown your AI agents
          already generate - the status lines, phase headings, decision
          tables and due dates scattered across your repos, parsed and
          shown as one overview instead of re-read one file at a time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Note what the DIY setup's own centerpiece is for: CLAUDE.md files
          brief the <em>agent</em> at session start. Nothing in that loop
          briefs <em>you</em>. The gap <Sitrep /> fills is the human-side one -
          and it reads your CLAUDE.md files along with everything else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="table">
      <h2 id="table" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Side by side</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"><Sitrep /></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium">VS Code + CLAUDE.md</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map(({ dim, sitrep, other }) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 text-left font-medium">{dim}</th>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted">{sitrep}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 text-ink-muted">{other}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-diy">
      <h2 id="choose-diy" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When the DIY setup is enough</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You run one or two projects and touch them most days - the state
          fits in your head because it never has time to fall out.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Your agents produce little markdown beyond code - there is not
          much state to lose track of.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You genuinely maintain your CLAUDE.md and plan files, and the
          discipline holds. Some people's does.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Adding any tool feels heavier than the problem. That instinct is
          usually right - respect it until the problem grows.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        If that is you, keep the free setup. <Sitrep />'s zero-adoption-cost
        promise cuts both ways: because it changes nothing about your files,
        there is also nothing to prepare before adopting it later.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-sitrep">
      <h2 id="choose-sitrep" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where the DIY setup stops scaling</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Projects multiply. Five active repos means five heads' worth of
          state in one head; re-entry starts costing the first fifteen
          minutes of every session, and by Friday the fragments add up to a
          missing day.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Agent output outgrows reading. A few weeks of Claude Code, Cursor
          or Codex leaves hundreds of plans, analyses and decision docs -
          more state than any file tree communicates.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          The docs start lying. A plan says phase 2 while the repo moved on;
          a decision was superseded three files later. Nothing in an editor
          flags it - staleness surfacing needs a layer that knows what the
          docs claim and when everything last moved.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Decisions go missing. "Waiting on you" is a state no file tree
          shows - it has to be extracted from the documents and surfaced, or
          it silently ages.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="both">
      <h2 id="both" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Adding <Sitrep /> changes nothing</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-ink-muted">
        This is the shortest migration section ever written, because there
        is no migration: you keep VS Code, you keep your CLAUDE.md files,
        you keep every workflow you have. <Sitrep /> points at the folders you
        already use and parses what is already in them - no import, no
        frontmatter, no restructuring. If it earns nothing at a glance, you
        delete it and your files never knew it was there. The <a href="/how-it-works/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">conventions it reads</a> are the ones your agents already write.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-faint">
        <Sitrep /> is in development, launching first on macOS. Local-first -
        your files never leave your machine.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
